@page "/forbidden"
@layout MainLayout

@* Where a signed-in operator lands when an address needs a role they do not hold. Says the three
   things they can act on — who the tool thinks they are, which role the address wanted, and which
   address — because the fix is somebody else's: whoever maps the provider's groups to the tool's
   roles, and the operator has to tell them all three. *@

<PageTitle>Not allowed</PageTitle>

<h1>Not allowed</h1>

<AuthorizeView>
    <Authorized>
        <p>
            You are signed in as <strong>@context.User.Identity?.Name</strong>, and
            <code>@ReturnUrl</code> needs the <strong>@Role</strong> role, which you do not hold.
        </p>
    </Authorized>
</AuthorizeView>

<p>
    Roles are granted by mapping a claim your sign-in carries to the tool's roles in its
    configuration. Ask whoever runs this tool to map yours, or go <a href="">back to the home page</a>.
</p>

@code {

    /// <summary>The role the address needed, as the redirect said.</summary>
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "role")]
    public string? Role { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Where the operator was going, as the redirect said.</summary>
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "returnUrl")]
    public string? ReturnUrl { get; set; }

}
